= Halo effect =

== Overview ==

{{:Halo effect/Simple}}

The '''halo effect''' concerns the influence of an overall evaluation on judgments of specific attributes, often introducing common-rater bias. The psychological effect should be distinguished from the legitimate correlation of underlying traits. An observer rating several attributes similarly may be influenced by a global impression, but correlated ratings alone are not enough to identify that cause.

== Sources and study designs ==

Edward Thorndike (1920) examined correlated ratings and described the tendency for a general appraisal of a person to color separate trait judgments. Nisbett and Wilson (1977) conducted experiments probing how an overall impression could alter more specific evaluations and the adequacy of people's introspective explanations.<ref name="thorndike_core">Edward L. Thorndike, [https://doi.org/10.1037/h0071663 “A Constant Error in Psychological Ratings”], ''Journal of Applied Psychology'' 4, 25–29 (1920).</ref><ref name="nisbett_core">Richard E. Nisbett and Timothy D. Wilson, [https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.35.4.250 “The Halo Effect: Evidence for Unconscious Alteration of Judgments”], ''Journal of Personality and Social Psychology'' 35(4), 250–256 (1977).</ref>

== What counts as evidence of bias ==

A study should identify independent information about the evaluated attribute, manipulate or measure a global impression and establish whether an irrelevant dimension changes the attribute judgment. Blind independent assessment, repeated ratings and objective criterion measures improve identification. In applied settings, some dimensions are genuinely correlated, so the goal is not to force zero correlation but to distinguish valid information from rater contamination.

{| class="wikitable"
! Explanation !! What it predicts !! Distinguishing information
|-
| Shared underlying trait || Ratings correlate because abilities or behaviors actually covary || Independent objective measurement and multi-rater agreement
|-
| Global impression bias || Unrelated impression affects specific ratings || Random manipulation of irrelevant cues
|-
| Response-style artifact || A rater systematically uses high or low scale values || Rater fixed effects, scale checks and randomized anchors
|}

== Relation to the broader knowledge graph ==

The halo effect connects to [[Related to::Cognition|cognitive judgment]], [[Related to::Reasoning|inference]], [[Related to::Metacognition|awareness of decisions]] and [[Related to::Decision theory|evaluation]]. No single shared trait-correlation pattern proves any one of these mechanisms.

